1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 15, the final end to end release.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Here is the whole workflow from the project report.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
A developer commits on a feature branch and merges into develop.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Jenkins checks out the branch, installs, lints and runs all the tests.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
If any test fails, the build fails and the deploy is skipped.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:35,000
If all are green, Jenkins archives the source with a checksum, builds the Docker images with the version and commit tag, pushes them to the registry, deploys and checks the health.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Ansible then provisions a server and can roll back.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
I will now verify each link with real data.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Link one and two: the commit and the checkout.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
This is the commit at the tip of develop.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:02,000
And this is what Jenkins built in its most recent build, build number twelve: the log line from the checkout stage names the same commit and the branch.

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
The build name has the version and the commit.

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
The command reads the admin password from the environment file, without displaying it.

14
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Links three to six: installing, linting, testing and the quality gate.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The stages of that build, every one a success.

16
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,000
And the log lines that prove the tests: eighty five unit and integration tests passed, five Selenium tests passed.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:47,000
The quality gate is this ordering: if either test stage had failed, everything after it would have been skipped, as we saw with the deliberate defect in Task 10.

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Link seven: the source archive and the checksum.

19
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
I do not just trust the checksum file, I verify it.

20
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
This command downloads the archive and its checksum file from Jenkins, computes the SHA two five six of the archive myself, and compares.

21
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
If they match, the archive that Jenkins stored is exactly what it recorded.

22
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Then it deletes the temporary files.

23
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Links eight to eleven: the image, the registry, the deployment and the health check.

24
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The registry has the image for this exact tag.

25
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The running staging container was created from the image with the same id as the registry's tag.

26
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,000
And the health address reports the version and the commit of this build, the same as the tip of develop.

27
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
So what was tested is what is running.

28
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Link twelve: Ansible.

29
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Task 13 and 14 ran the playbook and the rollback live on the lab server.

30
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:18,000
These are the saved results of the earlier runs, unedited: first provisioning, seven changes, the upgrade, five, the repeat run, zero changes, the rollback, three changes, and five of five Selenium journeys passing on the restored release.

31
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
The lab is a container standing in for a server.

32
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
It was not tested on a real virtual machine.

33
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:31,000
The documentation.

34
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:50,000
The project report has a troubleshooting table of the problems that really happened: certificate errors caused by the antivirus, Docker Desktop not running after a reboot, missing secret keys, ports in use, Selenium and Chrome mismatches, and Ansible permission problems.

35
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Each has the cause and the fix.

36
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Here are the symptoms.

37
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
The known limitations, stated honestly.

38
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
SQLite means one server.

39
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Plain HTTP in the demo.

40
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Rollback recreates containers, so there is a few seconds of downtime.

41
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Jenkins polls instead of using a webhook.

42
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Docker Hub publishing was not run.

43
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Ansible was verified on a container, not a real virtual machine.

44
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:34,000
And this older note about GitHub is out of date: the repository was pushed, as the Task 4 video showed, but issues and pull requests do not exist yet.

45
00:04:39,000 --> 00:05:03,000
And what I would improve next: PostgreSQL for several app servers, HTTPS with Let's Encrypt, automatic expiry of pending bookings, a staff dashboard, e-mail confirmations, a GitHub webhook with a multibranch pipeline, blue green deployments for zero downtime, monitoring of the health endpoint, and image vulnerability scanning in the pipeline.

46
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:20,000
For the viva I prepared a cheat sheet with a thirty second introduction, a two minute explanation and the workflow in order, and for every task a file of likely questions with answers specific to this project.

47
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Here is the thirty second introduction, and the number of viva question files in this demonstration pack.

48
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
To end, what is still open, taken from the project checklist.

49
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:48,000
The items marked prepared need something only I can do: the GitHub issues and pull requests need my GitHub login, Docker Hub needs an account, and a real Ansible run needs a virtual machine.

50
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Everything else is verified, and this video showed the chain from a commit to a verified deployment, with real data.


